### Project-specific context

Both codelines (mocka and mockb) are ESM TypeScript projects (`"type": "module"`, `target: ES2022`, `moduleResolution: "bundler"`, `strict: true`, `noEmit: true`). The `tsconfig.json` in each codeline includes both `src` and `test`. Tests are co-located under `test/` and import source via relative paths (e.g. `import { fareFor } from '../src/fares'` in mocka, `import { formatStops, totalMinutes } from '../src/schedule'` in mockb). Each codeline defines three npm scripts: `test` runs `vitest run`, `build` runs `tsc --noEmit`, and `lint` is a no-op (`echo no-lint-configured`). No lint configuration exists; do not expect lint feedback.

For mocka (`/home/bradleyjerome/projects/mock3/mock-a`): the fare boundary defect is in the `fareFor` function in `src/fares.ts`. The function currently uses `rider.age > 65` for the senior concession boundary, but the JSDoc and specification say riders aged 65 and over pay the concession fare — so the operator must be `>=`. The constants `BASE_FARE_CENTS` (350) and `CONCESSION_FARE_CENTS` (175) are already exported and used by the existing tests in `test/fares.test.ts`. The `Rider` type (`{ age: number; hasPass: boolean }`) and `totalFor` helper are also exported from the same module.

For mockb (`/home/bradleyjerome/projects/mock3/mock-b`): the departure-board defect is in the `formatStops` function in `src/schedule.ts`. The loop bound is `i < stops.length - 1`, which omits the final stop from the rendered string. The fix is to iterate through all stops (`i < stops.length`). The `totalMinutes` function reads `stops[stops.length - 1].minutes` and is not itself defective — do not change it. The `Stop` type (`{ name: string; minutes: number }`) is exported from the same module. Existing tests in `test/schedule.test.ts` import both `formatStops` and `totalMinutes`.

When the implementer (typescript-vitest-engineer) works on either codeline, run `npm test` from the codeline root to execute vitest, and `npm run build` to type-check with `tsc --noEmit`. Both must pass before a change is considered complete.

